f 1063 #edb/ref 17592186173151 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172880 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186172928 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172880 #edb/ref 1061 30461 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172880 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "9a817a13-cf12-5a56-abd1-7fbe139fe7b4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172880 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186172023 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172881 #edb/ref 1061 30463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172881 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "9b6bd47e-dc8a-5923-bbcc-1f0390d2d1db" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172881 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186172024 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172928 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186172881 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172928 #edb/ref 1061 32450 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172928 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "213cee54-dfad-5696-a210-97d588e61069" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172928 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186173170 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173151 #edb/ref 1064 "When finding the roots of a transformed radical function, we need to consider whether the radical's index is odd or even.\n\nLet's start with the odd case first. As an example, we will find the root of the function $f(x)$, given by\n\n$$\nf(x) = \\sqrt[{\\color{blue}{3}}]{10-4x} + 2\\,\n$$\n\nNotice that we're dealing with a cube root, and so the index of the radical is ${\\color{blue}{3}}$, which is odd.\n\nTo find the root of $f(x)$, we must solve\n\n$$\nf(x)=0\n$$\n\n$$\n\\sqrt[3]{10 - 4x} + 2 = 0\n$$\n\nWe solve this equation by making $x$ the subject, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sqrt[3]{10 - 4x} + 2 &= 0 \\\\\n\\sqrt[3]{10 - 4x} &= -2 \\\\\n(\\sqrt[3]{10 - 4x})^{3} &= (-2)^{3} \\\\\n10 - 4x &= -8 \\\\\n- 4x &= -18 \\\\\nx &= \\frac{9}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the root of $f(x)$ is\n\n$$\nx=\\dfrac92\n$$\n\nA transformed radical function with an odd index will always have one real root." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173151 #edb/ref 1065 4864 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173151 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "a49268e2-e58c-5966-ba98-b1786827ba76" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173151 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173170 #edb/ref 1064 "Let's now learn how to find the root of a radical function with an even index. For example, let's try to find the root of\n\n$$\nf(x) = -3\\sqrt[4]{10x+1} + 9\n$$\n\nTo find the root of $f(x)$, we must solve\n\n$$\nf(x)=0\n$$\n\n$$\n-3\\sqrt[4]{10x+1} + 9 = 0\n$$\n\nWe solve this equation by making $x$ the subject, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-3\\sqrt[4]{10x + 1} + 9 &= 0 \\\\\n- 3\\sqrt[4]{10x + 1} &= -9 \\\\\n\\sqrt[4]{10x + 1} &= 3 \\\\\n(\\sqrt[4]{10x + 1})^{4} &= 3^{4} \\\\\n10x + 1 &= 81 \\\\\n10x &= 80 \\\\\nx &= 8\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we must check the answer by substituting it back into the initial equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-3\\sqrt[4]{10 \\cdot 8 + 1} + 9 &= 0 \\\\\n- 3\\sqrt[4]{81} + 9 &= 0 \\\\\n- 3 \\cdot 3 + 9 &= 0 \\\\\n0 &= 0✓\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the root of $f(x)$ is $x=8$.\n\nWhen the index of the radical function is even, we should always check for extraneous roots by substituting them back into the original equation.\n\nSometimes, a radical function with an even index has no root. Let's see an example." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173170 #edb/ref 1065 5161 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173170 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "0a1feb60-62da-5279-8bfd-f8be6c389de7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173170 #edb/ref 1067 "Finding the Roots of a Transformed Radical Function: Even Case" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186429071 #edb/ref 1030 1.0 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186429071 #edb/ref 1032 "Initial Codex estimate from captured target-band crossing frequency. Selected account foundations: 1 rises / 1 eligible passed source activities = 1.000000; nearest selected fraction = 1. Crossing activity numbers [1]; waits [1]. Separate account estimates: foundations=1 (1/1). Selection: most observations, then lower weight, then account nam